WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.530
A canyon wall records events that happened before the canyon existed.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.710
Most sediment begins in nearly horizontal layers.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.630
Each new layer rests on material already there, so an undisturbed

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:17.170
sequence gets younger upward. Original horizontality and superposition answer different questions:

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.930
how beds began, and which bed came first.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.890
Dune slopes and overturned beds require care.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.950
A piece of basalt must exist before erosion can put it

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:30.410
into a younger deposit. The same logic applies when a solid

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.690
fragment falls into magma and becomes an inclusion in granite.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:37.210
Follow the fragment from its original rock into its new host.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.210
The fragment is older; the surrounding rock forms later.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.570
A joint, fault, vein or dike can cut material that already

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.010
exists. The cutting feature is therefore younger than the rock it

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.470
actually cuts. Trace the contact rather than extending it in your

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:55.050
imagination. A dike that stops below a younger bed does not

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.230
demonstrate that it also intruded that bed.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:01.090
Magma can bake and chemically alter its older surroundings.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.430
An intrusive sill between existing beds can heat rock above and

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.510
below. A lava flow can bake the ground beneath it, while

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.530
sediment deposited after it cools remains unbaked.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:15.630
Crosscutting and contact effects are independent clues that can support the

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:16.750
same age relationship.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.150
Sketch the five tools together:

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:24.010
original horizontality, superposition, inclusions, crosscutting and contact effects.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.410
For each interpretation, name the observation that supports it.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.550
These principles establish relative order;

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.230
they do not supply a numerical age on their own.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.570
Later folding, erosion and renewed deposition can add more events than

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:37.930
there are visible rock layers.
